{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>וַיַּצִּילֵהוּ מִיָּדָם.</b> Meaning: since man possesses free choice and will, and can kill one who has not been liable to death — unlike wild beasts, which will not harm a person unless he has been liable to death in Heaven — therefore it says <b>וַיַּצִּילֵהוּ מִיָּדָם</b>: meaning, from the hand of their free choice. And thereby he countered their saying ״וְנִרְאֶה מַה יִהְיוּ חֲלוֹמוֹתָיו״ and so forth, for free choice can nullify the matter, and there would be no proof if they killed him, for he would have spoken falsely." "<b>לֹא נַכֶּנּוּ נָפֶשׁ.</b> Meaning: not directly, but we will bring about his death. And this is what he said afterward: <b>אַל תִּשְׁפְּכוּ דָם הַשְׁלִיכוּ</b> and so forth. This was his argument to them, while the reason concealed within him was to save him, to return him to his father — for he knew with certainty that the beast of the field was forbidden to him, and that serpents and scorpions would do no harm and no destruction to the seed of Jacob, nor would famine detain him there to die. And as you will indeed find, immediately ״וַיָּשָׁב רְאוּבֵן אֶל הַבּוֹר״ — he returned to it to bring him up from the pit."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:40.314039114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}